        "analysis": {
          "topic": "AI工程",
          "summary": "Most of AI is pointed at language. Anima Anandkumar points it at the physical world, weather, fusion, materials, the systems physics writes down as equations, and finds the usual playbook breaks. There isn't enough data, the resolution is brutal, and no transformer is big enough. Her way through is older than deep learning: build the structure of the world into the model. It already produces weather forecasts that rival the supercomputers on a single GPU.\n\nCo-founder of Accelerated Understanding, Anima has spent two decades in AI, from the theory that predates deep learning, through scaling it at AWS and NVIDIA, and back to first principles. We get into neural operators, Fourier layers, and the spherical harmonics behind FourCastNet 3, then follow the same ideas into fusion reactors, chip design, and formal verification, before closing on her seat on the UN Scientific Advisory Board and why AI for science shouldn't be regulated like a chatbot.\n\nNote: we did a brief followup to this interview to dig into more details on Accelerated Understanding after the launch:    • Trillion Token Context. No, Really — Anima...  \n\n\nCHAPTERS\n0:00 Cold open and intros\n3:48 Anima's thesis: AI for science, not language models\n6:44 TorchLean: proving what a neural network will do\n12:06 Why physics-informed nets fail, and how neural operators fix it\n16:08 What a neural operator actually is\n19:41 Fourier neural operators, and why Fourier\n26:53 Old math meets deep learning, and why scaling breaks\n32:39 Weather, and the 2021 skeptics\n36:13 One model for weather and climate: assume a sphere\n42:40 Why the physical world is forgiving\n46:19 Modeling across scales, from atoms to planets\n50:33 The data, ECMWF, and calling Hurricane Lee early\n57:48 Long rollouts, and why the sphere stays stable\n1:04:06 Fusion: a plasma digital twin\n1:07:59 Career full circle: theory, scale, and back to principled\n1:13:02 A foundation model for physics, and inverse design\n1:18:13 The UN, regulating AI for science, and a call to action\n\nLINKS\n\nPapers\nFourCastNet: https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11214\nNeural Operators: https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.08481\nFourier Neural Operator: https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.08895\nFourCastNet 3: https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12144\nTorchLean: https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22631\nLean: https://lean-lang.org\n\nAnima Anandkumar\nCaltech: https://www.eas.caltech.edu/people/anima\nUN Scientific Advisory Board: https://www.caltech.edu/about/news/an...\n\nThe show\nLatent Space: https://www.latent.space",
          "tags": [
            "AI工程"
          ]
        },
